== Children Giving Back to Parents

=== §. 39. #emph[Of children’s recompense.]

The general head to which belong all the duties children owe their parents with respect to their #emph[Necessity] is, in one word, #emph[Recompense.] It is a duty by which children endeavor, as far as lies in them, to repay what they can for their parents’ kindness, care and cost toward them, and that by way #footnote[οὐθὲν ποιήσας ἄξιον τῶν ὑπηργμένων δέδρακεν. #emph[Arist. Ethic. l.] 8. #emph[c.] 14. \[He has done nothing worthy of what he has received.\]] of thankfulness. This makes a child think he cannot do too much for his parent; and well may he think so, for a parent does much more for his child before it is able to do anything for itself than the child can possibly do for the parent. So even if the parents’ #emph[authority] were laid aside, the law of #emph[fairness] would still require this duty of #emph[Recompense;] and so do the law of #emph[piety] and the law of #emph[charity.] Therefore, of all duties, this is the most due. #footnote[1 Tim 5:4 ἀμοιβὰς ἀποδιδόναι.] The apostle expressly charges children with it, bidding them “learn” to “requite their parents.”

Contrary to this is the neglect of parents in their need, which is more #footnote[It is monstrous ingratitude for children to neglect their parents.] than monstrous ingratitude. As all ingratitude is odious to God and man, so this is most of all; and yet very many are guilty of it. In them the proverb is proved true that #emph[love is heavy.] For it is the property of heavy things to fall down quickly but to rise slowly, and not without some force. So love falls down quickly from the parent to the child, but it rises only with difficulty from children to parents. For this reason another proverb says, #emph[One father will better provide for nine children than nine children for one father.] Many children in this respect do no more for their parents than for strangers. Either they do not consider how much their parents have done for them, or else they imagine that what their parents did was mere duty, and needs no recompense. Shame on such barbarous and inhuman children!

=== §. 40. #emph[Of infirmities to which parents are subject.]

The rule of the recompense named above is, on the one side, #footnote[A rule of recompense.] the #emph[parent’s Necessity,] and on the other, the #emph[child’s Ability.] So in everything in which a parent needs his child’s help, the child must give his best help, as far as he is able. #emph[Beyond one’s power nothing can be expected.] #footnote[Ultra posse non est esse.]

A parent’s #emph[Necessity] may arise from

- #emph[Natural infirmities.]
- #emph[Chance misfortunes.]

Natural infirmities are

- #emph[Inward.]
- #emph[Outward.]

#emph[Inward] infirmities are weakness of judgment, a slippery memory, violent passions and the like, from which come perversity, irritability, suspiciousness, distrust, fear, grief, etc.

#emph[Outward] infirmities are such as arise from some pressing temptation, as were Noah’s#footnote[Gen 9:21.] and Lot’s#footnote[Gen 19:33.] drunkenness; Lot’s#footnote[Gen 19:33.] and David’s#footnote[2 Sam 11:4.] uncleanness; Abraham’s#footnote[Gen 12:13.] and Isaac’s#footnote[Gen 26:7.] pretense; Jacob’s#footnote[Gen 37:34, 35.] and David’s#footnote[2 Sam 18:33.] excessive lamentation, etc.

Some of these latter, which may seem most heinous and odious sins, are to be counted infirmities when those who commit them do not make a sport of them, nor delight to live and lie in them, as swine wallow and lie in the mire, but only at times, through some temptation, fall into them, as it were unawares; and after they have committed them, they are themselves more ashamed of them and more grieved for them than anyone else who sees them or hears of them.

With respect to the natural infirmities of parents, the duty of children is both to #emph[bear with them] and also to #emph[cover them] as far as they can.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of children’s bearing with their parents’ infirmities.]

Children bear with their parents’ infirmities when they do not esteem their place or person any less reverently, nor perform any less duty to them, because of their infirmities.

This is the first particular branch of recompense. For children in their younger and weaker years are subject to many infirmities. If parents had respected them the less for their infirmities, and had taken occasion from them to neglect them, and would not have borne with them, surely they could not have been brought up so well. The great patience, long-suffering and much forbearance which parents have shown toward their children require that children, by way of recompense, show the like to their parents as occasion arises. It was a great infirmity in Isaac to prefer Esau, a profane child, to Jacob, a religious child, especially against God’s express word concerning Jacob; yet Jacob did not respect his father one bit the less for it, as appears by his #footnote[Gen 27:12.] fear of offending him, and by his #footnote[Gen 28:5.] readiness to obey him. Jacob’s#footnote[Gen 37:10.] unjust reproof of Joseph was no small infirmity, and yet the history that follows shows how much Joseph reverenced his father and respected him in every way. Saul’s infirmities were far more and much greater than any of theirs, yet what duty and faithfulness Jonathan his son showed him, even to their deaths! For #footnote[1 Sam 31:2.] he died with him.

We have in this the pattern of Christ himself. How great an infirmity his mother showed when she too rashly rebuked him while he was about a good work, a bounden duty, his Father’s business! Yet immediately after this it is noted that he “went down” with his parents “and was obedient to them.”#footnote[Luke 2:51.] This shows the honor he gave to his mother, in spite of her infirmity.

Those act contrary to this duty who take occasion from their parents’ infirmities to think basely of their person and #footnote[A child must not despise a parent for any infirmity.] their place, and so grow careless in duty, either refusing to do any duty at all, or else doing it carelessly, grudgingly, disdainfully and scornfully. Absalom#footnote[2 Sam 15:3.] made a supposed infirmity of his father the ground of his rebellion. Even if his pretext had been true, it would not have been sufficient cause for him to disgrace his father and rise against him, as he did. The law that threatens God’s vengeance against children whose eye “mocks a father and scorns obedience to a mother”#footnote[Prov 30:17.] makes no exception for parents’ infirmities.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of children’s covering their parents’ infirmities.]

Children cover their parents’ infirmities both by passing them by (as we say) and taking no notice of them, and also by concealing them from others as much as they can. Scripture notes it as a property of #emph[love] that it “covers over a multitude #footnote[1 Pet 4:8.] of sins.” Now in whom should love abound, if not in children? And who should show this property of love more than children?

Of passing by a parent’s infirmity and concealing it from others we have a worthy pattern in Shem and Japheth. When Noah their father lay drunk and uncovered in the middle of his tent, they, “walking backward” (so that they themselves might not #footnote[Gen 9:23.] see their father’s infirmity), “covered their father’s nakedness” (so that others might not see it). The blessing then promised on this occasion to them and their descendants shows how acceptable this duty was to God.

Contrary to this was Ham’s practice: he exposed and made #footnote[A sin for a child to expose a parent’s infirmity.] known his father’s nakedness.#footnote[Gen 9:22. #emph[v.] 25.] The curse denounced against him for it shows how odious that sin was to God. There are too many of Ham’s cursed brood who spread abroad their parents’ infirmities and make such things known about them as would not otherwise be known. By this they bring much dishonor and shame on their parents (which can be no honor #footnote[#emph[Parentis imprecatione nihil perniciosius excogitari potest. Plato de Leg. lib.] 11. \[Nothing more ruinous can be devised than a parent’s curse.\]] to the children), and besides, a curse from their parents on themselves, which the heathen counted very dreadful.

Still more contrary was Absalom’s practice: he raised a most malicious slander against his father, and by it turned his subjects’ hearts away from him. Too many, like Absalom, seek to raise a supposed #footnote[More than monstrous for a child to raise a slander against a parent.] reputation and honor for themselves by vilifying and disgracing their parents. But let them note Absalom’s end. Assuredly, if they persist in that course, a like end, or a worse, will be theirs.#footnote[2 Sam 15:3.]

=== §. 43. #emph[Of children’s bearing with their parents’ chance needs.]

Chance misfortunes are all kinds of crosses that are laid on a man by the providence of God: whether on his body, as blindness, lameness, sickness, etc.; or on his person, as captivity, banishment, imprisonment, etc.; or on his means, as poverty, destitution, etc. In all these children must bear with their parents, as in the infirmities named above, neither esteeming them less reverently nor performing less duty because of them. These are needs that are not sinful in themselves; and therefore, with respect to these, parents are to be borne with much more. Though Isaac was #emph[blind,]#footnote[Gen 27:1. Ruth 1:16, 21.] Jacob did not respect him one bit the less. Though Naomi was poor, Ruth her daughter-in-law continued to do a child’s duty and service to her.

Contrary to this is the heartless disposition of children who #footnote[Heartless to despise a parent for any chance infirmity.] take occasion from these chance needs of their parents to despise them. God has made an express law against despising those who are made helpless by any outward defect,#footnote[Lev 19:14.] as the #emph[deaf, blind,] etc. If no one may despise another for these, much less may children despise their parents. Those who do so are worse than Ham, and may expect a heavier curse.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of children’s relieving their parents according to their need.]

Besides bearing with their parents’ needs, children must give their parents relief and help in such cases as the parents need it from them. In sickness they must visit them, as Joseph#footnote[Gen 48:1.] visited his father. In time of mourning they must comfort them, as #footnote[Gen 37:35.] the children of Jacob did. In need they must provide what is needful for them, as #footnote[Gen 42:3.] the sons of Jacob did, who went to buy food for their father; and as Joseph#footnote[Gen 47:12.] did, who sent for Jacob into Egypt and there provided for him. It is noted of Ruth#footnote[Ruth 2:18.] that she not only gleaned for her mother, a poor woman, but also saved for her some of the food that was given to herself to eat. In time of danger they must do what they can for their parents’ protection and preservation, as David#footnote[1 Sam 22:3, 4.] in this respect took special care of his father and mother. Indeed, if God is pleased to #footnote[Vetustissima lex erat inscripta mentibus omnium, vt liberi si hoc officij grauentur impendere quod pulli ciconiarum reponunt suis parentibus, velut impij conijciantur in vincula. Erasm. Instit. Matrim. \[There was a most ancient law written in the minds of all, that children who begrudged rendering the duty which the young of storks pay back to their parents should be cast into chains as impious.\]] take children out of this world before their parents, and their parents are left without help, the children must make what arrangements they can for their parents’ well-being after their own departure, as #footnote[John 19:27.] Christ did, who entrusted his mother to his disciple John a little before his death. These and similar duties are particular branches of #emph[recompense,] and are all included under that #footnote[1 Tim 5:4 ἀμοιβὰς ἀποδιδόναι.] #emph[requital] which the apostle requires of children. And they are only a small part of the requital of all the pains, care and expense that parents have gone to for their children. Indeed, this one thing alone, that parents have brought children into the world, children can never sufficiently requite. Nature has taught this much not only to heathen men, but also to irrational creatures. Among such creatures the example of the #footnote[Arist. Plin. Suidas.] #emph[stork] is worth noting. For it is recorded of that kind that when the parent birds are old, the young ones feed them; and when through age they are ready to faint in their flying, the young ones help them; and when they are past flying, the young ones carry them on their backs. The #footnote[πελαργός. ἀντιπελαργεῖν. Sin of Pharisees in freeing children from relieving their parents.] Greek name of a #emph[stork] is taken from that #emph[word] which means to #emph[requite a parent’s kindness;] or else this word is taken from that name: they both come from the same root.

Contrary to this is the opinion of the Pharisees, who thought that children, by dedicating their substance to the temple, might be freed from this duty of #emph[recompense] to parents; this is what lies hidden in that Hebrew word #emph[Corban.]#footnote[Mark 7:11.] They made a mere pretext of piety toward God the cause of open impiety against parents. Christ gives this verdict on them: “you#super[+] nullify the word of God.”#footnote[#emph[v.] 13.] #footnote[See §. 14. 1. Sin of children in denying relief to parents.] The papists are of the same opinion, and so under the same condemnation.

Contrary also is the practice of those who, having the goods of this world, allow their parents to be in need. Saint John#footnote[1 John 3:17. #emph[Iniquissimum est patrem egere, cùm filius sit in facultatibus. Digest. lib.] 25. #emph[tit.] 3. §. 5. \[It is most unjust that a father should be in need while his son is well off.\]] asks of one who “withholds his compassion from” his brother in that case, “how can the love of God abide in him?” How then can it abide in such a child? Not only those who let their parents starve offend in this extreme, but also those who let them live poorly and wretchedly while they themselves, #footnote[Luke 16:19. 2. In bringing parents into extremities.] like Dives, live in luxury and go splendidly dressed every day.

Those offend in a higher degree who bring their parents into extremities: into poverty by their lavish spending; into prison by pressing them to be their sureties; into excessive grief by their wicked doings, as #footnote[Gen 37:34. 3. In striking parents.] the sons of Jacob did.

But what shall we say of such graceless children as dare to strike their parents? #footnote[Exod 21:15. 4. In murdering parents.] God’s law counts such a child unworthy of life, and sentences him to death.

And what can be said of #emph[killers of fathers] and #emph[killers of mothers?] Of old there was no particular law made against them, because it was supposed that no child could be so heartless and inhuman. In imitation of this, Solon,#footnote[Solon cùm interrogaretur, cur nullum supplicium constituisset. in eum qui parentem necasset, respondit, se id neminem facturum putasse. Cic. pro Ros. Amer.] that wise lawmaker among the heathen, made no law against them; and when he was asked his reason, he answered that he thought no child would commit such a deed.

When such inhuman impiety later showed itself in the world, the civil law ordained this punishment: “If anyone kills his parent, let him not be put to the sword, nor to fire, nor to any other usual punishment; but let him be sewn into a sack with a dog, and a cock, and a viper, and an ape, and cast into the nearest sea or river; so that while life is in him, he may begin to lack all use of the elements, and be deprived of the air while he lives, and of the earth when he is dead.”#footnote[#emph[Si quis parentis fata properauerit, neque gladio, neque ignibus, neque vlli alij solenni poenae subiugetur, sed insutus culeo cum cane, & gallo gallinaceo, & vipera, & simia, vel in vicinum mare, vel in amnem proijciatur, etc. Justin. Cod. l.] 9. #emph[tit.] 17. #emph[Cur voluerint parricidas insui in culeum viuos, atque ita in flumen deijci, docet Cicero pro Ros. Amer.] \[Why they would have parricides sewn alive into a sack and so thrown into a river, Cicero shows in his speech for Roscius of Ameria.\] #emph[Ad famam filij attinet, vt videatur honorem debitum patri] defuncto #emph[praestitisse. Cic. Philip.] 9. \[It concerns a son’s good name that he be seen to have paid the honor due to his dead father.\]]

Since this sin had been committed among the heathen, the apostle lists it among other most notorious and barbarous sins, 1 Tim 1:9. As murder is one of those sins which the earth can least bear, and which cries loudest to heaven for vengeance, so among the various kinds of murder this is the most unbearable, and cries the loudest.

So much for the duties of children which they are to perform while their parents live. It remains to speak of those which they are to perform when their parents are dead.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of children’s care to bury their parents when they are dead.]

The duties which children owe to their parents after they are dead concern the #emph[body] and the #emph[reputation] of their dead parent.

It is the duty of children to bring the bodies of their dead parents to the grave with such seemliness and honor as may correspond to the place and reputation in which they lived.

So both the #emph[thing] itself and the #emph[manner] of doing it are to be observed. #footnote[Burial a means to maintain hope of the resurrection.]

The thing itself, namely the #emph[burial] of the bodies of the dead, has always been in use in God’s church, and it has been used as a means to maintain our hope of the resurrection of our bodies. Many of the heathen, who never dreamed of the resurrection, #footnote[#emph[Constituere pyras: huc corpora quisque suorum More tulere patrum, subiectisque ignibus, etc. Virg. Aeneid.] 11. \[They built pyres; to them each man brought the bodies of his own, after the custom of their fathers, and with fires set beneath, etc.\]] used to burn the dead bodies of their relatives; other heathen learned this manner of burial from the church, though they did not know its mystery. It is clearer than needs proving that God’s people, from the beginning of the world, have performed this duty of burial for their relatives; but it is not to the point in hand to dwell on the general. That it belongs especially to children to see this duty performed is now the point to be proved, and it is readily done by the approved examples of Isaac,#footnote[Gen 25:9.] Jacob,#footnote[Gen 35:29.] Joseph#footnote[Gen 50:7.] and others expressly recorded in Scripture.

And there is great reason for it:

1\. It is a testimony of great love and good respect to the one #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.]] who has died. Now who should show more love and greater respect than a child?

2\. It is a kind of #emph[blessing]#footnote[1 Kgs 14:13. 2 Kgs 22:20.] promised by God to his saints #emph[to] #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason.]] #emph[be buried,] just as, on the other side, it is a #emph[curse]#footnote[Jer 22:19. 1 Kgs 21:23, 24. Ps 79:3.] threatened against obstinate sinners #emph[not to be buried.] For this reason David#footnote[2 Sam 2:5.] blesses the men of Jabesh-gilead for burying Saul, and acknowledges it as a kindness done to Saul. Now who ought rather to procure a blessing for parents and do them a kindness than children, who are often blessed through their parents?

3\. Since it is a great shame to have a man’s corpse lie above #footnote[3\. #emph[Reason.]] ground (for no carcass is more loathsome than a man’s if it lies unburied), children, who are most bound to cover their parents’ shame, are in this respect bound to bury their bodies. #footnote[Sin of children in neglecting their parents’ dead bodies.]

Contrary to this is the practice of those whose minds are so set on their parents’ goods that they wholly neglect their bodies. As soon as their parent’s breath is out of his body, they busy themselves so much about the things he has left behind that his corpse is ready to stink before any care is taken for its burial. Indeed, some will purposely keep their parent’s corpse above ground until it is extremely offensive, in order to receive some revenues, or debts, or other dues which must be paid before the corpse is buried. If the corpse must of necessity be kept above ground for some time, let it be embalmed, or so treated that it does not smell. Those who are careless of this show that they respect their parent’s wealth #footnote[Sin of children taking over their parents’ estate before they are dead. 1 Kgs 1:5.] more than his person and honor.

For this reason those also transgress heinously who are so greedy for their parent’s estate that they cannot wait for his departure, but, like Adonijah, enter on their father’s estate and take possession of his goods before the breath is out of his body. By this they often cause great disquiet to him who would depart in peace.

Again, others bear an inward grudge and secret hatred #footnote[Sin of children disturbing their parents’ funeral.] against a brother or other kinsman whom their parent dearly loved, and for that reason dared not lay a hand on him in their parent’s lifetime. As soon as their parent is dead, they pick a quarrel with the one they hate, and so disturb and hinder their parent’s funeral. Esau intended such a plot,#footnote[Gen 27:41.] but God defeated it, which shows that God is displeased with it.

=== §. 46. #emph[Of the seemliness with which children ought to see their parents buried.]

The #emph[manner] in which children ought to see their parents buried must be with such #emph[seemliness] as agrees with the commendable custom of the country and church where their parents die, and with such #emph[honor] as in some measure corresponds to the rank and place of their parents while they lived (at least if it is not beyond the means the parent has left, or beyond the ability of the child who holds the funeral). Joseph was a great ruler in Egypt, and because of this his father was highly regarded when he came there; accordingly #footnote[Gen 50:7.] he carried him to his grave with great honor.

There are two extremes contrary to the #emph[seemliness] #footnote[Contrary: 1. Too lavish funerals.] and #emph[honor] named above. One is an overlavish and prodigal extravagance and ceremony at their parents’ funeral, far above the parent’s rank and far beyond the means the parent has left, and far above the rank and ability of the child himself too. Some are cast so far into debt by the needless ceremony of their parents’ funeral that they are never able to recover themselves again, and so they bring more dishonor to their parents by the weakness of their own means than honor by the ceremony of the funeral. There may be great honor and much seemliness in a funeral where there are no extraordinary expenses: take Stephen’s funeral, for example. #footnote[Acts 8:2.]

The other extreme is too lowly and private a manner of #footnote[2\. Too lowly funerals.] burying their parents, very unbecoming both to their parents and to their own rank and means. It arises from a mixture of pride and covetousness possessing their hearts. Pride makes them have no ceremony at all, because covetousness will not allow them to spend freely on it. So it comes about that they choose out unfamiliar places where neither their parents nor they themselves are known, and the dead of night, so that no one may see them; and they set an uncertain time, so that no friend may go with them. God often repays such proud, covetous children in kind, and causes them to be brought to their graves with the same dishonor.

=== §. 47. #emph[Of children’s paying their parents’ debts after their death.]

As children must have regard for the body of their parents #footnote[Children’s regard for the reputation of their dead parents.] who have died, so also for their credit and good name, which is a thing of greater worth and honor: a thing by which they may bring a kind of blessing to their parents and make them live after their death.

Parents themselves cannot do anything when they are dead to preserve it; children therefore, being the living image of their parents, must endeavor to do it.

There are three things which children must make conscience of for the sake of their dead parents’ credit and reputation: one, to pay their debts; another, to suppress ill rumors; a third, to imitate their good example.

I. If the estate of parents, their goods or lands, comes to their children, the children’s duty is to pay their parents’ debts as far as they can, especially if those debts may be recovered from them by law. For what the law may force others to do in fairness and justice, conscience must move good children to do out of charity and as recompense to their parents. The Holy Spirit makes it a #footnote[Ps 37:21.] mark of the wicked that they “borrow and do not repay.” Therefore, to wipe that blot from the name of a dead parent, children must be ready in this respect to do what the parent himself, if he were living, would or should do. Indeed, if children are well able of themselves, they ought to pay all their parents’ debts, even though their parents did not leave enough to pay them. In this especially a childlike love is shown to the parent.

Contrary to this is the practice of those who strive to get all they can of their parents’ goods, and yet make no conscience of paying any debts at all unless the law forces them to. What they do in this case cannot be thought to be done for their parents’ sake, but rather for their own sake. Many have so little regard for their parents’ reputation in this respect that they secretly convey away and wholly conceal much of their parents’ estate, on purpose to cheat creditors. As this is a piece of plain injustice, so it is a cause of opening men’s mouths against their parents, to their discredit and shame.

=== §. 48. #emph[Of children’s suppressing evil reports against their dead parents.]

The direction given before concerning children’s speech #footnote[§. 7.] about their parents behind their backs may also fitly be applied to the care which children ought to have of the speeches and reports made about their parents after their departure. It follows, as #emph[from the less to the greater,] that what children do for their parents’ reputation in their absence, behind their backs, they must do much more when their parents are dead. For then there is no hope, no possibility, that parents should do anything to right their own wrong in that respect: it lies on children, therefore, to do it.

They act flatly contrary to this who take occasion from their parents’ departure both to open their ears to receive any ill reports of them and to open their mouths to speak ill of them, spreading abroad all their infirmities and stretching their ill reports of their parents beyond the bounds of truth. They are ill birds that so foul their own nest. They know that their parents, being dead, can have no knowledge of it; and in this they show how little piety toward God or parent is in their hearts. But there is an ever-living, all-seeing and all-knowing Father who takes notice of all; and besides other vengeance, he will cause the same measure to be measured out to them as they measure out to their parents. There is nothing in which this proverb (“with the measure you#super[+] use, it will be measured #footnote[Matt 7:2.] to you#super[+]”) is more often proved true than in children’s ingratitude #footnote[#emph[Quaecunque parentibus stipendia intuleris, eadem ipse a filiis expecta. Thal. Miles. apud Diog. Laert. l.] 1. \[Whatever support you give your parents, expect the same yourself from your children.\]] to their parents. Every age has given many instances of it. (The very heathen observed it.) This shows God’s great indignation against it.

=== §. 49. #emph[Of children’s imitating their parents’ good example.]

If parents have been persons of good conduct in their lifetime, religious toward God, just in their dealings with men, merciful to those who needed their help, doing much good in their place, and so ended their days with much credit, it is a special means of maintaining and continuing this credit of theirs for children to walk in their steps and to endeavor to be like them.

In this way a blessed memory of their parents is kept fresh and green (as we say), though their bodies have rotted. For when those who knew the parents see the like good qualities and actions in their children, they are reminded by it of the departed, and say, “Oh, how such parents still live! See a vivid and living image of them.” In this way Solomon, Asa, Jehoshaphat, Hezekiah, Josiah and other such good kings who came of the stock and lineage of David kept the memory of their father David fresh, fair and flourishing (long after his body had rotted), as is evident by these and similar phrases: he “walked in the statutes of his #footnote[1 Kgs 3:3.] father David”; he “walked in all the ways of his father David”;#footnote[2 Kgs 22:2.] he “did what was right … as his father David had done,”#footnote[1 Kgs 15:11.] etc. There can be no better monument of a parent’s piety, uprightness and virtue than a child’s vivid likeness of them. Therefore, #footnote[#emph[Nullum monumentum clarius relinquere potuit, quàm effigiem morum suorum, virtutis, constantiae, pietatis filium. Cic. Philip.] 9.] as a motive to stir up children to walk in the good ways of their parents, God has promised to show “loving devotion to a thousand generations of those who love Me and keep My commandments”; that is, to those who, having religious and righteous parents, walk in their steps.

Contrary to this are both those who are unlike good parents and those who are like evil parents. The former sort greatly damage and dishonor the reputation of their parents, as Rehoboam did, who by his foolish, harsh and unjust conduct made the people speak contemptuously of David. #footnote[1 Kgs 12:16.]

The latter sort keep alive in memory the evil name and the shame of their parents, and make them stink more and more: as the sons and successors of Jeroboam did, who by following his idolatrous course made it the more remembered, and caused this stain to remain on his title from age to age: “Jeroboam, who made Israel sin.” #footnote[1 Kgs 15:26.]

As #emph[the former] stop the current and hinder the passage of the blessing of righteous parents, so #emph[the latter] spread and open a way for the curse of unrighteous parents.

=== §. 50. #emph[Of the superstitious duty imposed by papists on children after their parents’ death.]

To the two duties named above, #emph[burying the body] and #emph[preserving the reputation] of parents, the #emph[papists] add a third and the #emph[heathen] a fourth, neither of which is warranted by God’s word, but both are directly contrary to it.

What the #emph[papists] add is that children, after their parents’ #footnote[#emph[Bellar. Tom.] 2. #emph[Contr.] 3. #emph[De Purgator. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 16. #emph[etc.]] death, ought to procure #emph[dirges, Masses, pardons, releases] and such trifles for them, and make continual prayers to free them out of purgatory and bring them to rest in heaven. Of the emptiness of these prayers and the folly of the other trifles I have #footnote[In #emph[Whole Armour of God,] Treatise 3, §. 39. 40, on Eph 6:18.] spoken elsewhere.

Scripture expressly teaches that after death the soul goes to the place appointed for it, of endless bliss or woe, so that there is no means of altering the one or of helping forward the other.

=== §. 51. #emph[Of the unlawfulness of children’s seeking to avenge their parents’ wrongs.]

What the #emph[heathen] add is that children, after their parents’ #footnote[Seneca in Agamen. Poetae tradiderunt filios patris vlciscendi causa supplicium de matre sumpsisse, idque deorum iussis, atque oraculis fecisse. Cic. pro. Sex. Ros. \[The poets have handed down that sons, to avenge their father, punished their mother, and did it at the command and oracles of the gods.\]] death, should avenge such wrongs as were done to their parents in their lifetime. And they press this so far on children that they frighten them with their parents’ #emph[ghost,] saying that if they neglect to avenge their parents’ wrongs, the ghost will follow them and not let them live in quiet, but trouble them continually. This notion arises from the corruption of nature, which is extremely prone to revenge; but it is expressly forbidden in Scripture, in these and similar prohibitions: “Do not resist an evil person,”#footnote[Matt 5:39.] “Do not repay anyone evil for evil,”#footnote[Rom 12:17.] “Do not avenge ourselves”#footnote[Rom 12:19.] etc. Yet some, to justify it, cite David’s#footnote[1 Kgs 2:5, 8.] charge to Solomon to take vengeance on Joab and Shimei after his death.

#emph[Answer.] The charge which David gave, and Solomon’s carrying it out, was no matter of private revenge, but only a lawful execution of justice, which children may and ought to do. There were just reasons to move David to put off the execution of justice on the one and the other so long. Joab#footnote[2 Sam 3:39.] was too mighty to have justice executed on him in David’s time; and Shimei’s #footnote[2 Sam 19:23.] offense was committed in the time of David’s humbling, which made him swear that he would not himself take vengeance on him. Besides, Solomon executed justice on the one and the other, not for the wrongs they did to his father, but for other crimes which they committed in his own time: their former offenses were only remembered to add weight to the case.

So much for children’s #emph[duties.] The #emph[manner] of performing them is to be declared next.
